# RME clinic visit — authority from the EXPLICIT actor (rule 199)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/audit-rme-clinic-visit-branch-authority-1.md`.
Extends rule 198 (invoice) and rule 197 §E (payment) to the visit domain.
**Supersedes rule 198 §31** — that residual is CLOSED. Rule 198 §30 (the shared
`RmeWorkingBranchScope::branchIdsFor(null)` null contract) is NOT changed and stays
recorded: this rule makes ClinicVisitService unable to pass null, it does not
change what the shared resolver answers for null.

## A. What the audit found (reproduced on the unmodified base `a98af8fc`)
1. `ClinicVisitService` read `Auth::user()` in 8 places and `BranchContext` in 1:
   list/queue/worklist/count scope (`:94`), the branch selector (`:143`), the
   doctor auto-assignment actor (`:251`), `created_by` (`:312`), the doctor branch
   lock on create (`:505`), the lock-refusal audit (`:554`), "Selesai
   Pemeriksaan" (`:661`), the doctor visit scope (`:726`); `find()` read
   `BranchContext` and had ZERO callers.
2. LATENT (no non-HTTP caller exists): with no session the lists, queue, counts
   and selector answered EVERY RME branch (fail-open); the ambient session, not
   the intended actor, decided scope; "Selesai Pemeriksaan" SKIPPED the clinical
   check for a null user; `create()` failed only at the database (NOT NULL).
3. EXPLOITABLE over HTTP (authenticated, insider): `GET rme/visits/patient-options`
   authorized only `viewAny`, so a Kasir pinned to branch A read branch B's
   patient visit history (number, date, type, doctor, initial treatment, status)
   — HTTP 200. The RME dashboard summed today's payments, receivables, cashier
   pending, month counts and draft records over EVERY RME branch for a
   context-bound operator, and `?branch_id=` reached any branch.
3a. EXPLOITABLE over HTTP, pre-existing since Sprint 20 (found by the independent
   review): `UpdateClinicVisitRequest` accepted `clinic_room_id` and `doctor_id`
   checked only for existence. A crafted PUT moved a branch-A visit into a
   branch-B room — bypassing same-branch, active-room, terminal-visit and
   doctor-from-room — or set ANY doctor, widening that doctor's patient scope.
   The edit form never sent either field. Reproduced RED (`room 3 instead of 1`).
3b. Production calibration (read-only, 2026-10-22): the ONLY active account with
   `view_clinic_visits` but not `manage_clinic_visits` is Owner (governance,
   already estate-wide). So F1 (picker) protects no account that exists today —
   Kasir was merged into Front Office, a registrar. F1 is a real code-level hole
   for any future view-only role, NOT a live exposure. F2 (dashboard) and 3a
   (edit) ARE reachable by current context-bound operators. 0 inactive accounts,
   0 inactive accounts with a session.

## B. The contract
4. Every actor-dependent ClinicVisitService method takes a NON-NULLABLE
   `User $actor`: `paginate`, `roomWorklist`, `registeredQueue`,
   `visitsTodayCount`, `waitingCount`, `inProgressCount`, `selectableRmeBranches`,
   `scopedBranchIdsFor`, `patientVisitOptions`, `create`, `assignRoom`, `update`.
   A null actor is a `TypeError` at the boundary. No default, no overload.
5. `transitionStatus(ClinicVisit, string, ?User $actor = null)` is the ONE
   nullable signature, and null is valid ONLY for `completed`: the cashier-owned
   transition, reachable only from `cashier_pending` (asserted) and only from
   `RmePaymentService` under its own explicit billing actor (rule 197). Every
   other target status requires a usable actor holding `transition`; "Selesai
   Pemeriksaan" (`cashier_pending`) additionally `completeExamination`. The old
   "system callers are not affected" skip is REMOVED — no system caller ever
   reached it. Refusals stay `ValidationException` on `status` (redirect, no 500).
6. Trusted actor source: the controller's `$request->user()`, behind `auth` and
   the existing policies. Never a request-supplied user id. Controller policies
   still run FIRST; the service check is independent and never weaker.
7. No ambient fallback: ClinicVisitService contains no `Auth::`, `auth()`,
   `app('auth')`, `request()`, `Request::`, `session()` or `BranchContext`
   (comment-stripping test pins it). The lock-refusal audit passes the explicit
   actor to `AuditLogService::log()`, whose own null fallback is
   `auth()->user()`.

## C. The single clinic-visit actor authority
8. `ClinicVisitActorGuard` (app/Modules/ClinicVisit/Services) is the ONE answer to
   "is this explicit actor a usable clinic-visit account?". `resolveUsable()`
   re-reads the row (persisted, key present, `is_active`, not soft-deleted) —
   same semantics as `RmeBillingActorGuard::resolveUsable()`, deliberately a
   different permission set.
9. Abilities are asked of `ClinicVisitPolicy` on the PERSISTED account, never
   restated as permission strings: `viewAny` (reads), `create` (registration,
   prior-visit picker), `update` (room, edit), `transition` / `completeExamination`
   (status). The guard and the controller can therefore never disagree.
10. Branch scope is asked of `RmeWorkingBranchScope` with the persisted account:
    `resolve()` for lists/counts (`scopedBranchIdsFor()`), `operationalBranchIdsFor()`
    for the selector, `allows()` for creation. Never re-derived.

## D. Fail-closed behaviour
11. Reads: an unusable or unauthorized actor gets an EMPTY scope (`whereIn([])`),
    zero counts and an empty selector — never the estate, MAIN or a first branch.
12. A request `branch_id` only NARROWS; outside the actor's scope it is ignored.
13. `create()`: a non-registrar is refused (`AuthorizationException`, 403) before
    branch resolution; a registrar is refused at a branch it is not working in
    (server-side twin of the FormRequest pin) before the doctor lock and before
    the transaction. A refused attempt writes NOTHING (no visit, no patient).
14. Mutations (`assignRoom`, `update`): refused with `AuthorizationException`
    unless the persisted actor holds `update` on that visit.
14a. Room and doctor are written ONLY by `assignRoom()` (`rme.visits.assign-room`),
    where the room rules live. `UpdateClinicVisitRequest` does not accept
    `clinic_room_id` / `doctor_id`, and `update()` strips `status`,
    `clinic_room_id` and `doctor_id` itself so a future caller cannot reintroduce
    the bypass. Never re-add them to the edit request.

## E. Visit history and aggregates
15. `patientVisitHistory()` / `adjacentVisits()` stay cross-branch BY DESIGN: their
    callers authorize the anchor visit first (patient-centric RM workspace,
    archive reads cross-branch, owner decision O3). Do not narrow them.
16. The prior-visit picker (`patientVisitOptions`) is REGISTRATION-only: `create`
    + `PatientSelectorSearchService::isSelectable()` — the same boundary the
    submit re-asserts. It is NOT narrowed to the registrar's working branch,
    because `StoreClinicVisitRequest::validateFollowUpVisit()` accepts a prior
    visit from ANY active RME branch. Whether cross-branch Kontrol stays a
    supported workflow is an OPEN OWNER DECISION (sprint doc §OD-1).
17. Any caller that aggregates visit-domain data itself (RME dashboard, visit-list
    widgets) takes its branch set from `ClinicVisitService::scopedBranchIdsFor()`,
    never from `BranchService::rmeEnabledIds()`. `MedicalRecordService::draftCount()`
    / `finalizedTodayCount()` take the branch set as a parameter.

## F. Preserved (verified by regression)
18. Doctor branch lock (rule 154) judged on the explicit registrar; refusal still
    audited once; branch_lock stays OFF in production.
19. Consent gate before "Selesai Pemeriksaan" (rule 109) unchanged and still the
    reason a consented-but-unsigned refusal names.
20. Invoice (rule 198) and payment (rule 197 §E) explicit-actor contracts,
    RmeBillingActorGuard, manual pricing D21 (rule 194), D10 payment block,
    invoice-version binding, snapshots, financial audit: untouched.
21. SPN4 correction pilot, maker user 29 / checker user 1 (rules 195–197): untouched.
22. Patient identity lookup global for registration (rule 101); doctor patient
    and room scope (rule 138); daily branch context lock (`92-daily-branch-context-lock.mdc`) unchanged.

## G. Recorded residuals (NOT changed here)
23. `RmeWorkingBranchScope::branchIdsFor(null)` still answers every RME branch
    (rule 198 §30). Controllers that hand `request()->user()` to the scope
    directly remain safe only inside `auth`.
24. No middleware enforces `is_active` per request (rule 198 §30a). Visit
    surfaces now fail closed for a deactivated account; policy-only surfaces do not.
25. `MedicalRecordService::paginate()` has no callers and still reads
    `Auth::user()` — MedicalRecord domain, separate audit candidate.
26. `MedicalRecordService::finalizedTodayCount()` uses UTC `now()` for "today"
    (rule 92 clinical-clock class) — separate fix.
27. `create()` does not re-assert patient selectability (the FormRequest does);
    no non-HTTP caller exists.
27a. Pre-existing N+1 on the context-bound visit list (~2 statements per visit:
    92 → 152 statements as visits go 10 → 40). Not touched.
27b. `transitionStatus(completed)` reads `status === cashier_pending` on the
    in-memory model without a row lock; the payment service holds its own lock.
27c. `show` / `edit` still render for a deactivated account with a live session
    (policy-only surfaces, rule 198 §30a).
27d. The guard is deliberately NOT memoised: ~3 statements per resolution, a
    constant (O(1)) addition per request (+17..+23 on the list, +9..+19 on the
    dashboard). A memo on a container- or route-cached object outlives the
    request — never add one to save these.

